use std
into vsel.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its The exported function `vs_bodies(n)` takes an i32 feature count and groups the features into bodies for the model tree, writing into `BO` for every feature i below n the index of the feature that starts its body, or minus 1 for a reference feature, or minus 2 for a feature that needs a body when none exists yet. `KD` gives each feature's kind (0 reference, 1 makes solid material, 2 changes existing material), `OP` its operation (0 add, 1 cut, 2 intersect, 3 new body) and `NS` the number of separate solids in the part right after it (minus 1 when unknown). Walking the features in order with cur equal to minus 1 and prev equal to 0: when KD[i] is 0, BO[i] is minus 1. When KD[i] is 1 and OP[i] is neither 1 nor 2: when cur is below 0, or OP[i] is 3, or NS[i] is below 0, or NS[i] is greater than prev, the feature starts a new body (BO[i] equals i and cur becomes i); otherwise BO[i] equals cur. In every other case BO[i] equals cur when cur is at least 0, otherwise minus 2. After each feature, when NS[i] is at least 0, prev becomes NS[i]. It returns the number of features that started a body.

The exported function `vs_comps()` splits the part mesh into connected solids and writes into `FC`, for every face below `NF`, its solid number (0, 1, 2 and so on, numbered in the order their lowest face index appears) or minus 1 for a face in no group; it returns the number of solids. Two faces are connected when they share a vertex position. Steps: find the bounding box of the `NV` vertices in `P` and let cell be the box diagonal times 1e-7 plus 1e-12. The hash table size S is the smallest power of two at least 2 times `NV` plus 2; `HK` gets `zi` of S and then every entry set to minus 1, `REP` and `OWN` get `zi` of `NV` (then every `OWN` entry is minus 1), and `UF` gets `zi` of `NF` with UF[f] equal to f. For every vertex v the quantized cell is qx, qy, qz, each the int of round of the coordinate divided by cell; the slot starts at the absolute value of (qx times 73856093 xor qy times 19349663 xor qz times 83492791) modulo S and probes linearly (slot plus 1 modulo S) until it finds an empty slot (`HK` minus 1), where it stores v and REP[v] becomes v, or a slot holding a vertex u whose quantized cell equals v's, where REP[v] becomes u. `find(f)` follows `UF` to the root with path halving (UF[f] becomes UF[UF[f]] at each step). Then for every group g below `NG` with start s, count c and face f from `GR` (skip the group when f is negative or not below `NF`), for every index entry k from s below s plus c: with r equal to REP[T[k]], when OWN[r] is minus 1 it becomes f, otherwise the roots find(f) and find(OWN[r]) are joined by setting `UF` of the larger root to the smaller root. A face is grouped when at least one group names it. Finally every `FC` entry starts at minus 1 and, walking faces f from 0 up, a grouped face whose root has no number yet gives that root the next number; FC[f] is its root's number (an i32 array of `NF` entries, all minus 1, holds the number per root).

The exported function `vs_cube(x, y, z)` takes a point on the surface of the orientation cube, which spans minus 1 to 1 on every axis, and picks the view direction for the face, edge or corner region under it: for each of the three coordinates, when its absolute value is greater than 0.55 the direction component is its sign (1.0 or minus 1.0), otherwise 0.0. When all three components are 0.0 the component of the coordinate with the largest absolute value becomes its sign. It writes the three components into OUT[0], OUT[1], OUT[2] (resizing `OUT` to 3 with `zf` and calling `vs_views` first when it holds fewer than 3) and returns the number of nonzero components.
The exported function `vs_bulge(ax, ay, bx, by, s, n)` turns one profile segment into points along it: from point (ax, ay) to point (bx, by) with signed sagitta s (a real) and an i32 segment count n. With L equal to the distance between the two points and h equal to the absolute value of s: when h is at most 1e-12 times (1 plus L), or L is at most 1e-12, or n is below 1, the segment is straight and it writes ax, ay, bx, by into OUT[0] to OUT[3] and returns 2. Otherwise with g equal to 1.0 when s is negative and minus 1.0 when it is not, the radius r equal to (L·L/4 plus h·h) divided by (2h), the unit right normal (qx, qy) equal to ((by minus ay)/L, (ax minus bx)/L), the center (cx, cy) equal to the chord midpoint plus g·(h minus r) times (qx, qy), the start angle t0 equal to atan2(ay minus cy, ax minus cx) and the sweep w equal to g times 4 times atan(2h/L), it writes n plus 1 points: point i (0 to n) is (cx plus r·cos(t0 plus w·i/n), cy plus r·sin(t0 plus w·i/n)) into OUT[2i], OUT[2i plus 1], and it returns n plus 1. Before writing, when `OUT` holds fewer than 2·(n plus 1) values (or fewer than 4 in the straight case) it is resized with `zf` to that size and `vs_views` is called.

`main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with vs_ is exported). It is the viewport selection engine of the part workspace: it projects part vertices and sketch-plane points to the screen through the camera, finds the faces a selection box catches, maps a screen pixel back onto a sketch plane, and works out which features have to go when the features they build on are deleted. Screen coordinates are pixels with x to the right and y down, measured from the top-left corner of the 3D view.

```amni
extern fn vs_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "vs_view_f64";
extern fn vs_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "vs_view_i32";
```

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `NV` (vertices), `NI` (index entries, three per triangle), `NG` (face groups), `NF` (faces), `NP` (points to project), `NK` (features). Reals, all starting at 0.0: `SW`, `SH` (screen width and height in pixels), `UX`, `UY`, `UZ` (the last point mapped back from the screen). Growable f64 arrays: `P` (3 per vertex), `M` (16 values, the camera view-projection matrix in column-major order like three.js Matrix4 elements), `MI` (16 values, its inverse, same order), `PL` (9 values: the sketch plane origin, its unit u axis and its unit v axis, each x, y, z), `IN` (2 per point), `OUT` (3 per point), `FP` (3 per face), `RC` (4 values), `SX` (3 per vertex). Growable i32 arrays: `T` (1 per index entry), `GR` (3 per group), `FS` (1 per face), `KD` (1 per feature), `DL` (1 per feature), `CS` (1 per feature), `OP` (1 per feature), `NS` (1 per feature), `BO` (1 per feature), `FC` (1 per face), `HK`, `REP` (1 per vertex), `OWN` (1 per vertex), `UF` (1 per face).

The exported function `vs_views()` registers every array JavaScript reads or writes: f64 views 1 `P`, 2 `M`, 3 `MI`, 4 `PL`, 5 `IN`, 6 `OUT`, 7 `FP`, 8 `RC` through vs_view_f64, and i32 views 9 `T`, 10 `GR`, 11 `FS`, 12 `KD`, 13 `DL`, 14 `CS`, 15 `OP`, 16 `NS`, 17 `BO`, 18 `FC` through vs_view_i32.

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to 0.0. `zi(xs, n)` takes an i32 growable array by reference and does the same with entries equal to 0 as i32. Every array in this file is sized only through these two helpers; no block defines other sizing helpers.

The exported function `vs_setup(nv, ni, ng, nf)` takes four i32 counts and stores them in `NV`, `NI`, `NG` and `NF`; with `zf` it sizes `P` to 3·nv, `SX` to 3·nv and `FP` to 3·nf, and with `zi` it sizes `T` to ni, `GR` to 3·ng, `FS` to nf and `FC` to nf. `M` and `MI` get `zf` of 16, `PL` gets `zf` of 9 and `RC` gets `zf` of 4 when their length is not already that size. It calls `vs_views` and returns 0.

The exported function `vs_points(np)` takes an i32 count, stores it in `NP`, sizes `IN` to 2·np and `OUT` to 3·np with `zf` (but at least 3 entries for `OUT`), fills `M`, `MI`, `PL` and `RC` with `zf` to 16, 16, 9 and 4 entries when their length is not already that, calls `vs_views` and returns 0.

The exported function `vs_feats(n)` takes an i32 count, stores it in `NK`, sizes `KD`, `DL`, `CS`, `OP`, `NS` and `BO` to n entries with `zi`, calls `vs_views` and returns 0.

The exported function `vs_screen(w, h)` takes two reals, stores them in `SW` and `SH` and returns 0.

`clipw(x, y, z)` returns the fourth clip coordinate of the point (x, y, z) through `M`: M[3]·x plus M[7]·y plus M[11]·z plus M[15].

`toscreen(x, y, z, o)` projects the 3D point (x, y, z) through `M` and writes the result into `OUT` starting at index o: with w equal to `clipw`(x, y, z), when w is at most 1e-9 it writes 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 (behind the camera); otherwise with cx equal to M[0]·x plus M[4]·y plus M[8]·z plus M[12] and cy equal to M[1]·x plus M[5]·y plus M[9]·z plus M[13], it writes (cx/w plus 1) times 0.5 times `SW`, then (1 minus cy/w) times 0.5 times `SH`, then 1.0.

The exported function `vs_proj(n)` takes an i32 count and, for every point i below n, reads plane coordinates u equal to IN[2i] and v equal to IN[2i plus 1], builds the 3D point PL[0] plus u·PL[3] plus v·PL[6], PL[1] plus u·PL[4] plus v·PL[7], PL[2] plus u·PL[5] plus v·PL[8], and calls `toscreen` on it with o equal to 3i. It returns 0.

`unm(nx, ny, nz)` maps normalized device coordinates (nx, ny, nz) back to a 3D point through `MI` and stores it in `UX`, `UY`, `UZ`: X equals MI[0]·nx plus MI[4]·ny plus MI[8]·nz plus MI[12], Y equals MI[1]·nx plus MI[5]·ny plus MI[9]·nz plus MI[13], Z equals MI[2]·nx plus MI[6]·ny plus MI[10]·nz plus MI[14], W equals MI[3]·nx plus MI[7]·ny plus MI[11]·nz plus MI[15]; when the absolute value of W is below 1e-12 W becomes 1e-12; it stores X/W, Y/W and Z/W.

The exported function `vs_unproj(x, y)` takes a screen pixel (two reals) and finds where the ray through it meets the sketch plane, writing u, v and an ok flag into OUT[0], OUT[1], OUT[2]. With nx equal to x/`SW` times 2 minus 1 and ny equal to 1 minus y/`SH` times 2, it calls `unm`(nx, ny, minus 1.0) and keeps a equal to (`UX`, `UY`, `UZ`), then calls `unm`(nx, ny, 1.0) and keeps b likewise. The plane normal n is the cross product of the u axis (PL[3], PL[4], PL[5]) and the v axis (PL[6], PL[7], PL[8]). With d equal to b minus a, den equal to n·d and num equal to n·(origin minus a) where origin is (PL[0], PL[1], PL[2]): when the absolute value of den is below 1e-12 it writes 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 and returns 0. Otherwise t is num/den; when t is below 0.0 it writes 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 and returns 0. Otherwise the hit point q is a plus t·d and it writes (q minus origin)·(u axis), (q minus origin)·(v axis) and 1.0, and returns 1.

The exported function `vs_scale(u, v)` returns how many screen pixels one plane unit spans near plane point (u, v). When the length of `IN` is below 6 or the length of `OUT` is below 9, `IN` is sized to 6 and `OUT` to 9 with `zf` and `vs_views` is called. With s equal to the larger of 1e-6 and 1e-3 times (1 plus the absolute value of u plus the absolute value of v), it writes IN[0], IN[1] equal to u, v, IN[2], IN[3] equal to u plus s, v, and IN[4], IN[5] equal to u, v plus s, and calls `vs_proj`(3). When OUT[2], OUT[5] or OUT[8] is 0.0 it returns 0.0. Otherwise it returns the average of the distance from (OUT[0], OUT[1]) to (OUT[3], OUT[4]) and the distance from (OUT[0], OUT[1]) to (OUT[6], OUT[7]), divided by s.

`inr(x, y)` returns true when x is between RC[0] and RC[2] and y is between RC[1] and RC[3], both inclusive.

`sat(o)` tests whether the screen triangle whose three corners sit in `SX` at vertex indices a, b, c (read as T[o], T[o plus 1], T[o plus 2]) overlaps the box in `RC`, returning true or false. When SX[3a plus 2], SX[3b plus 2] or SX[3c plus 2] is 0.0 it returns false. With corners (ax, ay), (bx, by), (cx, cy) taken from `SX` (x at 3k, y at 3k plus 1): when the largest of ax, bx, cx is below RC[0], or the smallest is above RC[2], or the largest of ay, by, cy is below RC[1], or the smallest is above RC[3], it returns false. Then for each of the three edges (a to b, b to c, c to a) with edge vector (ex, ey) and the normal (nx, ny) equal to (minus ey, ex): it projects the three triangle corners onto the normal (dot products) taking their minimum tmin and maximum tmax, projects the four box corners (RC[0], RC[1]), (RC[2], RC[1]), (RC[2], RC[3]), (RC[0], RC[3]) onto the normal taking bmin and bmax, and returns false when tmax is below bmin or bmax is below tmin. When no axis separates them it returns true.

The exported function `vs_box(x0, y0, x1, y1, mode)` takes the box corners (four reals) and an i32 mode (1 window: a face must lie fully inside; 0 crossing: touching is enough) and fills `FS` with 1 for every caught face and 0 otherwise. It stores the smaller of x0 and x1 in RC[0], the smaller of y0 and y1 in RC[1], the larger of x0 and x1 in RC[2] and the larger of y0 and y1 in RC[3] (the box is put in `RC` before projecting, and `toscreen` never touches `RC`). It then projects every vertex k below `NV`: it calls `toscreen`(P[3k], P[3k plus 1], P[3k plus 2], 0) and copies OUT[0], OUT[1], OUT[2] into SX[3k], SX[3k plus 1], SX[3k plus 2] (so `OUT` must hold at least 3 values: when its length is below 3 it is resized with `zf` to 3 and `vs_views` is called before the loop). Every entry of `FS` is set to 0 and every entry of `FP` to 0.0, and an i32 array of `NF` flags named seen (all 0) records which faces already have a point. For every group g below `NG` with start s equal to GR[3g], count c equal to GR[3g plus 1] and face f equal to GR[3g plus 2] (skip the group when f is negative or not below `NF`): the triangles of the group start at index entry s and there are c div 3 of them. When seen[f] is 0 and the group has at least one triangle, seen[f] becomes 1 and FP[3f], FP[3f plus 1], FP[3f plus 2] get the average of the three corners of the first triangle in 3D (from `P` using T[s], T[s plus 1], T[s plus 2]). In window mode the face starts as caught (a per-face i32 array ok of `NF` entries, all starting at 1 in window mode, is set to 0 for face f when any corner of any triangle of the group has SX flag 0.0 or is not `inr`); after all groups FS[f] is 1 exactly when f was in at least one group and ok[f] is still 1. In crossing mode FS[f] becomes 1 when `sat` is true for any triangle of the group (stop testing that group once one triangle hits). It returns the number of faces with FS equal to 1.

The exported function `vs_cascade(n)` takes an i32 feature count and works out which features must also go when the features flagged in `DL` are deleted. `KD` gives each feature's kind: 0 independent (datums and suppressed features), 1 makes solid material, 2 needs solid material made by an earlier feature. Walking the features in order from 0 to n minus 1 with a count made of 0: CS[i] is set to 0; when DL[i] is not 0 the feature is skipped; when KD[i] is 1, made grows by 1; when KD[i] is 2 and made is 0, CS[i] is set to 1. It returns the number of features with CS equal to 1.

`main()` does nothing.
